[FINAL-TEXT] I have all the evidence I need. Here is a summary of the confirmed findings:

| Device | dhcrelay running? | Process tree evidence |
|--------|-------------------|----------------------|
| dswa1 | ✅ Yes | `bash(1)→dhcrelay(147)` + FRR; cmd: `-i br0.10 -i br0.20 -i br0.40 -i br0.99 -i eth5 -i eth6 10.0.0.76` |
| dswa2 | ❌ No | `bash(1)` spawned only FRR + keepalived — **no dhcrelay child** |
| dswb1 | ✅ Yes | `bash(1)→dhcrelay(147)` + FRR; cmd: `-i br0.10 -i br0.20 -i br0.30 -i br0.99 -i eth5 -i eth6 10.0.0.76` |
| dswb2 | ❌ No | `bash(1)` spawned only FRR + keepalived — **no dhcrelay child** |

On dswa2 and dswb2, `/etc/default/isc-dhcp-relay` has empty `SERVERS=` and `INTERFACES=` fields, and the `isc-dhcp-relay` systemd unit is explicitly `disabled / inactive (dead)`. No alternative startup mechanism (`rc.local`, etc.) is present to compensate. On dswa1/dswb1 the relay was launched directly by the Kathara startup script (the `bash` PID-1 is the direct parent), not through systemd — this mechanism is simply absent from dswa2/dswb2's startup configuration entirely.

