=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa2, dswb2
diagnosis_text:
The DHCP relay subsystem (`dhcrelay`) is completely absent on two of the four distribution switches: **dswa2** and **dswb2**. On the working peers (dswa1 and dswb1), `dhcrelay` was launched at container startup by the Kathara startup script — it appears as a direct child of `bash(1)` in the process tree and correctly listens on all client-VLAN bridge subinterfaces (br0.10, br0.20, br0.30/40, br0.99) plus the uplinks to the core (eth5, eth6), forwarding discovers to the DHCP server at 10.0.0.76. On dswa2 and dswb2 the `bash(1)` PID spawned only the FRR routing daemons and keepalived — no `dhcrelay` process exists in either container. The `/etc/default/isc-dhcp-relay` config on both faulty switches has blank `SERVERS=` and `INTERFACES=` fields, and the systemd unit is `disabled / inactive (dead)`. No alternative mechanism (`rc.local`, etc.) compensates. The effect is that any DHCPDISCOVER broadcast that arrives exclusively at dswa2 or dswb2 — which occurs for VLANs on which those switches hold the VRRP virtual-IP (dswa2 is the VRRP active gateway for VLANs 20 and 40; dswb2 similarly for B-side VLANs) and for any access-switch uplink segment where STP has designated dswa2/dswb2 as the forwarding path — is silently discarded and never forwarded to the DHCP server. Existing leases continue because the lease renewal unicasts are routed successfully; only the initial broadcast-based DISCOVER from newly connected devices fails. Root cause: the Kathara startup scripts for dswa2 and dswb2 are missing the `dhcrelay` invocation that is present on dswa1 and dswb1, almost certainly omitted during the distribution-switch configuration change referenced in the ticket. Corrective fix: on dswa2 run `dhcrelay -i br0.10 -i br0.20 -i br0.40 -i br0.99 -i eth5 -i eth6 10.0.0.76`; on dswb2 run `dhcrelay -i br0.10 -i br0.20 -i br0.30 -i br0.99 -i eth5 -i eth6 10.0.0.76`; persist both commands in the respective `.startup` files.
=== END ===